\title{Exact Single-Bit Leakage of Fork Readouts:\\
A Universal Rational Profile, Its Phase Diagram,\\
and a Conservation Law}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{4 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{fork} model: $n+1$ independent Bernoulli($p$) bits
$x_0,\dots,x_n$ observed only through a single scalar readout $F$, with the
information about the designated bit $x_0$ measured by the squared Pearson
correlation, or \emph{leakage},
$\Leak_p(F) = \Cov(x_0,F)^2/(\Var(x_0)\Var(F))$.
Four readouts recur in the study of fanout channels: the conjunction (AND), the
disjunction (OR), the parity (XOR) and the split count (Hamming weight). We compute
all four exactly and prove that they are one rational function
$\Phi(t,n) = t^{n}/(1+t+\cdots+t^{n})$
evaluated at four channel parameters: $p$ for AND, $1-p$ for OR, $(1-2p)^2$ for XOR
and $1$ for the split count. Since $\Phi(\cdot,n)$ is strictly increasing for
$n \ge 1$, every ordering question reduces to a comparison of four numbers,
independently of the fork size. Consequently the split count dominates all Boolean
channels at every bias and size with exact value $1/(n+1)$; no size-dependent
crossover between channels can exist, refuting the reported ``AND overtakes XOR at
$n=8$''; and the ratio $X/g$ never tends to $2$, its behaviour being a trichotomy
pivoting on the critical bias $p=3/4$ at which XOR and OR coincide identically in
$n$. We further prove three structural theorems: every multiplicative readout
$\prod_i c(x_i)$ has leakage $\Phi(m^2/s,n)$ with $m=\Ex c$, $s=\Ex c^2$ and
$m^2/s<1$; every permutation-invariant readout obeys $\Leak_p(F)\le 1/(n+1)$ with
equality exactly for non-degenerate affine functions of the Hamming weight; and every
readout of an $N$-bit fork satisfies the conservation law
$\sum_i \rho_p^2(x_i,F)\le 1$, with slack equal to the normalised mean square of its
nonlinear part. Exact rational tables at $25$ bits close the numerical record.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The fork problem}

A great many measurement situations share one shape: a source of randomness fans out
into parallel components, a single scalar observation aggregates them, and one asks
how much of one designated component survives. Side-channel analysis asks it of power
traces combining register bits; feature attribution asks it of pooled activations;
sensor design asks it of any statistic summarising an array.

We call the abstraction a \emph{fork}. Its ingredients are a \emph{source} of $n+1$
independent bits $x_0,\dots,x_n$, each equal to $1$ with probability $p \in (0,1)$;
a \emph{readout} $F:\{0,1\}^{n+1}\to\mathbb{R}$; and a \emph{designated bit} $x_0$.
The four classical readouts are
\[
\AND(x)=\prod_i x_i,\quad
\ORr(x)=1-\prod_i(1-x_i),\quad
\XOR(x)=\tfrac12\Big(1-\prod_i(1-2x_i)\Big),\quad
w(x)=\sum_i x_i .
\]

Historically these were compared by tabulation. Tabulation is fragile: an
unnormalised weighting, or marginals inconsistent with the joint, yields
plausible-looking numbers with the wrong structure. Two conclusions read off such
tables --- a size-dependent crossover at $n=8$ and an asymptotic ratio $X/g\to2$ ---
are refuted below.

\subsection{The exact functional}

\begin{definition}[Fork weights and expectation]
For $p \in (0,1)$ and $x \in \{0,1\}^{N}$ set
$w_p(x)=\prod_{i}p^{x_i}(1-p)^{1-x_i}$ and
$\Ex_p[F]=\sum_{x}w_p(x)F(x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Master factorisation]\label{lem:master}
For coordinate functions $g_i:\{0,1\}\to\mathbb{R}$,
\[
\Ex_p\Big[\prod_i g_i(x_i)\Big]=\prod_i\big(p\,g_i(1)+(1-p)g_i(0)\big).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand the product of the $N$ two-term sums
$\sum_{b}(\text{weight of }b)\,g_i(b)$ by distributivity; the terms are in bijection
with bit patterns $x$, and the coefficient of $\prod_i g_i(x_i)$ is $w_p(x)$.
\end{proof}

Taking $g_i\equiv1$ gives $\Ex_p[1]=1$: the weights sum to one. This is proved, not
assumed --- it is the precise exclusion of the unnormalised-weight pathology.

\begin{definition}[Covariance, variance, leakage]
$\Cov_p(F,G)=\Ex_p[FG]-\Ex_p[F]\Ex_p[G]$, $\Var_p(F)=\Cov_p(F,F)$,
$\rho_p^2(F,G)=\Cov_p(F,G)^2/(\Var_p(F)\Var_p(G))$, and the \emph{leakage} is
$\Leak_p(F)=\rho_p^2(x_0,F)$.
\end{definition}

Leakage lies in $[0,1]$ and is invariant under $F\mapsto \alpha F+\beta$ with
$\alpha\neq0$: the units of the dial carry no information.

\begin{definition}[Channels and profile]
With $n+1$ bits, $A(p,n)=\Leak_p(\AND)$, $g(p,n)=\Leak_p(\ORr)$,
$X(p,n)=\Leak_p(\XOR)$, $\Is(p,n)=\Leak_p(w)$. The \emph{fork profile} is
\[
\Phi(t,n)=\frac{t^{n}}{\sum_{k=0}^{n}t^{k}}
        =\frac{t^{n}(1-t)}{1-t^{\,n+1}}\ (t\neq1),
\qquad \Phi(1,n)=\frac1{n+1}.
\]
\end{definition}

\section{The channel collapse}

\begin{theorem}[Channel collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
For every $p\in(0,1)$ and every $n\ge0$,
\[
A(p,n)=\Phi(p,n),\quad
g(p,n)=\Phi(1-p,n),\quad
X(p,n)=\Phi\big((1-2p)^2,n\big),\quad
\Is(p,n)=\Phi(1,n)=\frac1{n+1}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each case combines Lemma~\ref{lem:master} with the geometric identity
$1-t^{n+1}=(1-t)\sum_{k=0}^{n}t^{k}$ and $\Var_p(x_0)=p(1-p)$.

\emph{AND.} $\AND$ is idempotent and absorbs the designated indicator,
$x_0\AND=\AND$, while $\Ex_p[\AND]=p^{\,n+1}$. Hence
$\Cov_p(x_0,\AND)=p^{\,n+1}(1-p)$ and $\Var_p(\AND)=p^{\,n+1}(1-p^{\,n+1})$, so
$A=p^{n}(1-p)/(1-p^{\,n+1})=\Phi(p,n)$.

\emph{OR.} With $\NOR=\prod_i(1-x_i)$ we have $\ORr=1-\NOR$, $x_0\NOR=0$ hence
$x_0\ORr=x_0$, $\ORr^2=\ORr$, and $\Ex_p[\NOR]=(1-p)^{n+1}$. Thus
$\Cov_p(x_0,\ORr)=p(1-p)^{n+1}$, $\Var_p(\ORr)=(1-p)^{n+1}\big(1-(1-p)^{n+1}\big)$,
giving $g=\Phi(1-p,n)$.

\emph{XOR.} With $\pi=\prod_i(1-2x_i)$ we have $\XOR=(1-\pi)/2$, $\pi^2\equiv1$,
$\Ex_p[\pi]=(1-2p)^{n+1}$ and $\Ex_p[x_0\pi]=-p(1-2p)^{n}$. Hence
$\Cov_p(x_0,\XOR)=p(1-p)(1-2p)^{n}$ and $\Var_p(\XOR)=\big(1-(1-2p)^{2n+2}\big)/4$.
Writing $d=(1-2p)^2$ and using $1-d=4p(1-p)$ gives
$X=d^{n}(1-d)/(1-d^{\,n+1})=\Phi(d,n)$.

\emph{Split count.} By independence $\Cov_p(x_0,w)=\Var_p(x_0)=p(1-p)$ and
$\Var_p(w)=(n+1)p(1-p)$, so $\Is=1/(n+1)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Monotonicity and size-free orderings}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
Let $0\le s\le t$. Then $\Phi(s,n)\le\Phi(t,n)$; if $s<t$ and $n\ge1$ the inequality
is strict. Hence for $n\ge1$: $\Phi(s,n)\le\Phi(t,n)\iff s\le t$ and
$\Phi(s,n)=\Phi(t,n)\iff s=t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Cross-multiplying reduces the claim to
$s^{n}\sum_{k\le n}t^{k}\le t^{n}\sum_{k\le n}s^{k}$, which holds term by term
because $s^{n}t^{k}=s^{k}t^{k}s^{\,n-k}\le s^{k}t^{k}t^{\,n-k}=t^{n}s^{k}$ for
$k\le n$. For $n\ge1$ the term $k=0$ is strict unless $s=t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Order rigidity]
For $n\ge1$, the ordering of $A,g,X,\Is$ is the ordering of the parameters
$p,\,1-p,\,(1-2p)^2,\,1$, and hence does not depend on $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Split-count dominance]
For every $p\in(0,1)$ and every $n$,
$\max\big(A(p,n),g(p,n),X(p,n)\big)\le \Is(p,n)=1/(n+1)$, strictly for $n\ge1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Bias thresholds]
For $n\ge1$: $A\ge g \iff p\ge \tfrac12$, and $A\ge X \iff p\ge\tfrac14$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}, $A\ge g\iff p\ge1-p$ and $A\ge X\iff p\ge(1-2p)^2$, i.e.
$(4p-1)(p-1)\le0$, i.e. $p\ge1/4$ since $p<1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Crossover freedom]\label{thm:nocross}
Fix $p\in(0,1)$ and $m,n\ge1$. If $A(p,m)\le X(p,m)$ then $A(p,n)\le X(p,n)$, and
likewise for the pair $(A,g)$. No fork size at which one channel overtakes another
can exist.
\end{theorem}

Hence the reported crossover at $n=8$ is an artifact of the numerics, not a property
of the model.

\begin{theorem}[Universal decay]
For $0\le t\le1$ one has $\Phi(t,n)\le 1/(n+1)$, and for all $t\ge0$,
$\Phi(t,n)\le t^{n}$. Consequently every channel tends to $0$: the three Boolean
channels geometrically, the split count like $1/(n+1)$. Moreover
$(n+1)A(p,n)\to0$ and likewise for $g$ and $X$, so $A/\Is\to0$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The XOR/OR trichotomy}

\begin{theorem}[Ratio decay]\label{thm:ratio}
For $0\le s<t\le1$, $\Phi(s,n)/\Phi(t,n)\le (n+1)(s/t)^{n}\to0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Phi(s,n)\le s^{n}$ since the denominator is at least its $k=0$ term, and
$\Phi(t,n)\ge t^{n}/(n+1)$ since for $t\le1$ each of the $n+1$ denominator terms is
at most $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact degeneracy]
For $n\ge1$ and $p\in(0,1)$: $X(p,n)=g(p,n)\iff p=3/4$, and at $p=3/4$ the equality
holds identically in $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equality holds iff $(1-2p)^2=1-p$, i.e. $4p^2-3p=0$, i.e. $p\in\{0,3/4\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Refutation of the asymptotic ratio $2$]
For no $p\in(0,1)$ does $X(p,n)/g(p,n)$ converge to $2$. Precisely: for $p<3/4$ the
ratio tends to $0$ geometrically; at $p=3/4$ it is identically $1$; for $p>3/4$ it
diverges.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(1-2p)^2<1-p\iff p<3/4$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:ratio} in the appropriate direction
and uniqueness of limits.
\end{proof}

\section{Phase diagram}

\begin{theorem}[Four regimes]
For every $n\ge1$ the orderings are as follows.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
regime & ordering \\
\midrule
$0<p<1/4$ & $A<X<g<\Is$ \\
$1/4<p<1/2$ & $X<A<g<\Is$ \\
$1/2<p<3/4$ & $X<g<A<\Is$ \\
$3/4<p<1$ & $g<X<A<\Is$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Critical merges]
For every $n$: $A(1/4,n)=X(1/4,n)$, $A(1/2,n)=g(1/2,n)$, $X(3/4,n)=g(3/4,n)$; each
merge holds identically in the fork size.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Parity blindness]
For $n\ge1$ and $p\in(0,1)$: $X(p,n)=0 \iff p=1/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[No triple point]
For $n\ge1$ and $p\in(0,1)$ it is impossible that $A=g$ and $g=X$ simultaneously.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$A=g$ forces $p=1/2$, whereupon $X$ has parameter $0$ while $g$ has parameter
$1/2\ne0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact tables at 25 bits}

\begin{lemma}[Rational form]\label{lem:rat}
For $0\le a<b$, $\displaystyle \Phi\!\left(\frac ab,n\right)=\frac{a^{n}(b-a)}{b^{\,n+1}-a^{\,n+1}}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Closed 25-bit table]
At $p=1/3$ and $25$ bits (i.e.\ $n=24$),
\[
\Is=\frac1{25},\qquad
g=\frac{16777216}{847255055011},\qquad
A=\frac{1}{423644304721},\qquad
X=\frac{1}{89737248461481573596281},
\]
and consequently $X<A<g<\Is$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Is=1/(n+1)$. Lemma~\ref{lem:rat} with $a/b=1/3$ gives $2/(3^{25}-1)$; with
$a/b=2/3$ gives $2^{24}/(3^{25}-2^{25})$; with $(1-2/3)^2=1/9$ gives $8/(9^{25}-1)$.
\end{proof}

Numerically these are $4.0\times10^{-2}$, $1.98\times10^{-5}$,
$2.36\times10^{-12}$, $1.11\times10^{-23}$: twenty-one orders of magnitude separate
the weakest from the strongest channel, which is why floating-point tabulation is
qualitatively, not merely quantitatively, unreliable here.

\section{Product universality}

\begin{definition}
For $c:\{0,1\}\to\mathbb{R}$ let $P_c(x)=\prod_i c(x_i)$, with moments
$m=p\,c(1)+(1-p)c(0)$ and $s=p\,c(1)^2+(1-p)c(0)^2$, and product parameter
$\theta_p(c)=m^2/s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Moment gap]\label{lem:gap}
$s-m^2=p(1-p)\big(c(1)-c(0)\big)^2$. In particular, for non-constant $c$ and
$p\in(0,1)$, $0\le\theta_p(c)<1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Product universality]\label{thm:univ}
For $p\in(0,1)$ and non-constant $c$, on an $(n+1)$-bit fork
$\Leak_p(P_c)=\Phi\big(\theta_p(c),n\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:master}, $\Ex_p[P_c]=m^{\,n+1}$ and $\Ex_p[P_c^2]=s^{\,n+1}$, while
conditioning the designated coordinate gives $\Ex_p[x_0P_c]=p\,c(1)m^{n}$. Hence
$\Cov_p(x_0,P_c)=p(1-p)m^{n}\big(c(1)-c(0)\big)$ and
$\Var_p(P_c)=s^{\,n+1}-(m^2)^{\,n+1}$, so
\[
\Leak_p(P_c)=\frac{(m^2)^{n}\,(s-m^2)}{s^{\,n+1}-(m^2)^{\,n+1}}
=\Phi\!\left(\frac{m^2}{s},n\right)
\]
using Lemma~\ref{lem:gap} and Lemma~\ref{lem:rat} with $a=m^2$, $b=s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Re-derivation of the Boolean channels]
Since leakage is affine invariant: $c(1)=1,c(0)=0$ gives $\theta=p$ and
$A=\Phi(p,n)$; $c(1)=0,c(0)=1$ gives $\theta=1-p$ and, via $\ORr=1-\NOR$,
$g=\Phi(1-p,n)$; $c(1)=-1,c(0)=1$ gives $\theta=(1-2p)^2$ and, via
$\XOR=(1-\pi)/2$, $X=\Phi((1-2p)^2,n)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Structure of the product class]
Let $p\in(0,1)$, let $c,d$ be non-constant and $n\ge1$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\Leak_p(P_c)<1/(n+1)=\Is(p,n)$: no product readout matches the split count;
\item $\Leak_p(P_c)\to0$ geometrically at rate $\theta_p(c)<1$;
\item $\Leak_p(P_c)\le\Leak_p(P_d)\iff\theta_p(c)\le\theta_p(d)$, so no two product
      readouts ever swap order between fork sizes.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

The parameter $\theta_p(c)=m^2/s$ is the squared cosine of the angle, in the one-bit
inner-product space, between $c$ and the constant function: near-constant coordinate
functions leak almost as much as the counter, near-orthogonal ones almost nothing.

\section{Symmetric optimality}

\begin{definition}
$F$ is \emph{symmetric} if $F(x\circ\sigma)=F(x)$ for every coordinate permutation
$\sigma$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Equal covariances]\label{lem:eqcov}
If $F$ is symmetric then $\Cov_p(x_i,F)$ is independent of $i$, whence
$\Cov_p(w,F)=(n+1)\Cov_p(x_0,F)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Cauchy--Schwarz]\label{lem:cs}
$\Cov_p(F,G)^2\le\Var_p(F)\Var_p(G)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\lambda\mapsto\Var_p(F+\lambda G)$ is a non-negative quadratic; its discriminant is
$\le0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetric optimality]\label{thm:symopt}
For $p\in(0,1)$, every symmetric readout of an $(n+1)$-bit fork satisfies
$\Leak_p(F)\le 1/(n+1)$, and the Hamming weight attains the bound.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:cs} to $(w,F)$, substitute Lemma~\ref{lem:eqcov} and
$\Var_p(w)=(n+1)p(1-p)$ to obtain
$(n+1)^2\Cov_p(x_0,F)^2\le(n+1)p(1-p)\Var_p(F)$, and divide by
$p(1-p)\Var_p(F)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equality case]
For symmetric $F$ and $p\in(0,1)$,
$\Leak_p(F)=1/(n+1)$ if and only if $F=\alpha w+\beta$ for some $\alpha\neq0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Equality in Cauchy--Schwarz forces $\Var_p\big(F-\lambda w\big)=0$ with
$\lambda=\Cov_p(w,F)/\Var_p(w)$. All fork weights are strictly positive for
$p\in(0,1)$, so a readout of zero variance is constant; $\lambda\neq0$ since a
constant readout has zero leakage. The converse is affine invariance.
\end{proof}

Thus the split count is the unique optimal symmetric readout up to affine changes.

\section{The total-leakage sum rule}

\begin{definition}
For an $N$-bit fork let $z_i(x)=x_i-p$, and for a readout $F$ set
$\pi_i(F)=\Cov_p(x_i,F)/(p(1-p))$ and
$R_F(x)=F(x)-\Ex_p[F]-\sum_i \pi_i(F)z_i(x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Orthogonality and Pythagoras]
$\Ex_p[z_iz_j]=p(1-p)\delta_{ij}$ and $\Ex_p[Fz_i]=\Cov_p(x_i,F)$; consequently
\[
\Ex_p\big[R_F^2\big]=\Var_p(F)-\frac{1}{p(1-p)}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Cov_p(x_i,F)^2 .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Bessel's inequality for forks]
$\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Cov_p(x_i,F)^2\le p(1-p)\Var_p(F)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Total-leakage sum rule]\label{thm:sumrule}
For any readout $F$ of an $N$-bit fork, $\sum_{i=1}^{N}\rho_p^2(x_i,F)\le1$, and if
$\Var_p(F)>0$ then
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{N}\rho_p^2(x_i,F)=1-\frac{\Ex_p[R_F^2]}{\Var_p(F)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation family]
If $\Var_p(F)>0$, then $\sum_i\rho_p^2(x_i,F)=1$ if and only if
$F(x)=\beta+\sum_i b_i x_i$ for some coefficients $b_i$ and constant $\beta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Tightness]
The split count saturates the sum rule: $\sum_i\rho_p^2(x_i,w)=(n+1)\cdot\frac1{n+1}=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Leakage pigeonhole]
For any readout $F$ and any $\tau>0$,
$\#\{i:\rho_p^2(x_i,F)\ge\tau\}\le 1/\tau$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[At most one bit is half-leaked]
If $\rho_p^2(x_i,F)>1/2$ and $\rho_p^2(x_j,F)>1/2$ then $i=j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:sumrule} also gives a second, independent proof of
Theorem~\ref{thm:symopt}: a symmetric readout has all $N=n+1$ terms of the sum equal
to $\Leak_p(F)$, so $(n+1)\Leak_p(F)\le1$. This argument never mentions the Hamming
weight.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Exact channel table.}
Given rational $p=a/b$ and $n_{\max}$, evaluate $\Phi$ at $p$, $1-p$, $(1-2p)^2$, $1$
through $\Phi(a/b,n)=a^{n}(b-a)/(b^{\,n+1}-a^{\,n+1})$: $O(n_{\max})$ big-integer
operations per channel, replacing a $2^{\,n+1}$-term sum.

\paragraph{Brute-force certification.}
For small $n$, enumerate all $2^{\,n+1}$ patterns with exact rational weights and form
the covariances directly. Agreement with the closed form at every $n\le12$ certifies
the algebra; disagreement with a floating-point pipeline is the signature of an
unnormalised weighting.

\paragraph{Ordering oracle.}
To rank product readouts, compute the parameters $\theta=m^2/s$ and sort them: by the
product-class rigidity this ordering is correct at every fork size, at cost
$O(k\log k)$ independent of $n$.

\section{Applications and discussion}

\paragraph{Side-channel design.}
Among all permutation-invariant observables the Hamming weight is the worst possible
from a designer's point of view, and the equality case shows no reparametrisation
escapes it: only affine functions of the weight are that bad. In the other direction,
a single scalar observable can strongly compromise at most one bit.

\paragraph{Masking and parity.}
Parity is exactly blind at $p=1/2$; under bias its leakage is $\Phi((1-2p)^2,n)$,
quadratic in the bias gap and geometrically small in the fork size --- a sharp form of
the intuition that biased masks leak, but little, and less as the mask widens.

\paragraph{Pooling in learned models.}
Sum-pooling is affine in the indicators, hence saturates the sum rule and is the
maximally transparent pooling; multiplicative gates destroy first-order information
geometrically in fan-in. The sum-rule deficiency $\Ex[R_F^2]/\Var(F)$ is a natural
definition of a unit's nonlinearity: the fraction of its variance unexplainable by any
linear function of its inputs.

\paragraph{Methodological.}
The two mirages were not careless arithmetic but the output of a pipeline whose
weights did not sum to one and whose marginals were inconsistent with the joint. The
remedy is structural: write every expectation as an exact finite sum against explicit
product weights, prove $\Ex[1]=1$ rather than assume it, and impose complement forms
$\ORr=1-\NOR$, $\XOR=(1-\pi)/2$ as identities. The tables then need not be measured
at all.

\section{Future directions}

Beyond products and symmetry, a classification of the readouts realising a prescribed
leakage vector subject to the conservation law is open. Higher-order leakage --- about
sets of bits, via the biased Fourier--Walsh expansion --- is the natural next
hierarchy. For exchangeable but dependent sources the equal-covariance lemma survives
and hence a version of symmetric optimality, but the profile deforms. For general
i.i.d.\ alphabets with finite second moments we conjecture that the product
universality theorem holds verbatim with $\theta=m^2/s$. Finally, among readouts under
a complexity budget, how close to $1/(n+1)$ can a cheap readout get?

\section{Conclusion}

The four fork channels are one rational profile
$\Phi(t,n)=t^{n}/(1+t+\cdots+t^{n})$ read at four parameters: $p$, $1-p$, $(1-2p)^2$
and $1$. Because $\Phi$ is strictly increasing, all comparisons are size-free. This
confirms split-count dominance and universal decay, refutes the reported crossover at
$n=8$, and refutes the conjectured asymptotic ratio $2$ via the exact trichotomy about
$p=3/4$. Behind the collapse stand three structural theorems: multiplicative readouts
have leakage $\Phi(m^2/s,n)$ with parameter strictly below one; symmetric readouts
leak at most $1/(n+1)$, with equality exactly for non-degenerate affine functions of
the Hamming weight; and every readout distributes at most one unit of total squared
correlation among the source bits, with slack equal to its nonlinear content. The
tables are closed not because they were extended, but because they were replaced by a
formula.

\end{document}

